One common vulnerability in Java web applications is **Insecure Deserialization**, often caused by using the `ObjectInputStream.readObject()` method on untrusted data. This can allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code if certain "gadget chains" are present in the application's classpath.

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.net.ServerSocket;
import java.net.Socket;

public class VulnerableServer {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        ServerSocket serverSocket = new ServerSocket(8080);
        System.out.println("Server listening on port 8080...");

        while (true) {
            try (Socket clientSocket = serverSocket.accept();
                 ObjectInputStream ois = new ObjectInputStream(clientSocket.getInputStream())) {

                // VULNABLE: Deserializing data directly from an untrusted network socket 
                // allows an attacker to achieve Remote Code Execution (RCE).
                Object data = ois.readObject();
                System.out.println("Received object: " + data.toString());
            } catch (Exception e) {
                e.printStackTrace();
            }
        }
    }
}

